1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:03,210
Imagine a neighbor asks where they can get
help using

2
00:00:03,210 --> 00:00:07,370
a computer. You may already have a
familiar library, community

3
00:00:07,370 --> 00:00:12,090
center, or veterans' organization in mind.
Use that experience as

4
00:00:12,090 --> 00:00:16,410
a starting point, then verify what is
available now. Our

5
00:00:16,410 --> 00:00:20,010
task is to find a suitable public service,
record enough

6
00:00:20,010 --> 00:00:23,930
evidence to revisit it, and organize a
fictional handout without

7
00:00:23,930 --> 00:00:28,490
exposing private information. You can use
a different local service

8
00:00:28,490 --> 00:00:31,810
that matters to you. The names and details
in this

9
00:00:31,810 --> 00:00:35,950
demonstration are examples, not a claim
about any particular organization.

10
00:00:37,390 --> 00:00:40,970
A useful search ends with a supported
decision, not just

11
00:00:40,970 --> 00:00:42,170
a page that looks impressive.

12
00:00:44,237 --> 00:00:47,177
Start with the words that describe your
task, the kind

13
00:00:47,177 --> 00:00:51,777
of service, and the location. Computer
help library plus your

14
00:00:51,777 --> 00:00:56,057
town is more useful than typing help by
itself. Inspect

15
00:00:56,057 --> 00:00:59,597
the result title and the website address
before opening it.

16
00:00:59,997 --> 00:01:02,677
If the results are too broad, add a
specific service

17
00:01:02,677 --> 00:01:06,277
or location. If there are no useful
results, remove one

18
00:01:06,277 --> 00:01:10,317
restriction rather than assuming the
service does not exist. Filters

19
00:01:10,317 --> 00:01:12,817
can help, but they can also hide a
relevant page.

20
00:01:13,417 --> 00:01:16,577
Pause and write a search for your chosen
community task.

21
00:01:16,577 --> 00:01:19,957
Explain what each word contributes. Then
change one term and

22
00:01:19,957 --> 00:01:22,457
compare the results instead of opening
every link.

23
00:01:24,262 --> 00:01:27,422
A page can be accurate and still be wrong
for

24
00:01:27,422 --> 00:01:31,682
your decision. An official library page
from another town may

25
00:01:31,682 --> 00:01:34,822
describe a real service, but not the one
available to

26
00:01:34,822 --> 00:01:38,762
your neighbor. Ask who published the
information, what evidence

27
00:01:38,762 --> 00:01:42,402
supports it, when it was updated, why it
was written,

28
00:01:42,982 --> 00:01:47,002
and whether it answers your question. For
opening hours or

29
00:01:47,002 --> 00:01:50,802
eligibility, go to the organization
responsible for that service

30
00:01:50,802 --> 00:01:54,962
and check the details there. Search-result
snippets may be incomplete.

31
00:01:55,822 --> 00:01:59,442
Compare two pages that disagree. Do not
count votes between

32
00:01:59,442 --> 00:02:02,922
websites; identify which source is in a
position to know,

33
00:02:02,922 --> 00:02:07,682
and confirm uncertain details through an
independently found contact method.

34
00:02:09,396 --> 00:02:11,916
Suppose you find a useful service today
and need it

35
00:02:11,916 --> 00:02:15,336
again next month. A note saying good
website will not

36
00:02:15,336 --> 00:02:18,816
be enough. Record the organization, the
page title, the web

37
00:02:18,816 --> 00:02:21,816
address, the date you checked it, and the
particular fact

38
00:02:21,816 --> 00:02:25,136
you plan to use. A bookmark helps you
return, but

39
00:02:25,136 --> 00:02:27,396
it does not freeze the page or prove that
the

40
00:02:27,396 --> 00:02:31,096
information remains current. In your
handout, distinguish a checked

41
00:02:31,096 --> 00:02:34,576
fact from your own interpretation. If the
page does not

42
00:02:34,576 --> 00:02:38,796
answer a question, write needs
confirmation rather than filling

43
00:02:38,796 --> 00:02:41,756
the gap with a guess. This is the digital
equivalent

44
00:02:41,756 --> 00:02:45,096
of writing the source beside a number in a
household notebook.

45
00:02:47,007 --> 00:02:51,327
An online form is a structured request.
Read its purpose

46
00:02:51,327 --> 00:02:55,307
and required fields before typing. In our
course practice form,

47
00:02:55,667 --> 00:02:58,667
choose the kind of computer help needed
and a fictional

48
00:02:58,667 --> 00:03:02,907
contact preference. Review the entries
before selecting the final

49
00:03:02,907 --> 00:03:06,947
button. On a real service, ask whether a
sensitive detail

50
00:03:06,947 --> 00:03:10,487
is necessary for the task, and verify the
site before

51
00:03:10,487 --> 00:03:13,707
entering it. Our practice does not send a
real request.

52
00:03:14,527 --> 00:03:18,287
That distinction matters: seeing a
confirmation message in a simulator

53
00:03:18,287 --> 00:03:22,307
proves that you completed the exercise,
not that an organization

54
00:03:22,307 --> 00:03:26,867
received anything. Pause, complete the
fictional form, and explain

55
00:03:26,867 --> 00:03:29,127
what you deliberately left out and why.

56
00:03:30,864 --> 00:03:33,764
Think of a folder as a labeled place to
put

57
00:03:33,764 --> 00:03:37,824
related work. Create a practice folder for
the community resource

58
00:03:37,824 --> 00:03:44,444
task and use a descriptive file name, such
as library-help-draft.

59
00:03:44,444 --> 00:03:47,244
A file saved on this computer may not
appear on

60
00:03:47,244 --> 00:03:51,064
another computer. A cloud file belongs to
a service and

61
00:03:51,064 --> 00:03:54,164
an account, and a synced folder may keep
copies in

62
00:03:54,164 --> 00:03:58,124
more than one place. Before moving to
another device, check

63
00:03:58,124 --> 00:04:02,724
the account and the saving or syncing
status. Reopen the

64
00:04:02,724 --> 00:04:06,764
file from the intended location. That
simple test catches a

65
00:04:06,764 --> 00:04:12,084
common mistake: editing one copy while
later opening an older copy somewhere
else.

66
00:04:13,838 --> 00:04:17,758
Our partner should suggest clearer wording
without rewriting the

67
00:04:17,758 --> 00:04:23,518
handout directly. Viewer access usually
allows reading. Commenter

68
00:04:23,518 --> 00:04:28,578
access, when supported, allows suggestions
through comments. Editor

69
00:04:28,578 --> 00:04:33,398
access permits changes to the content.
Choose the smallest role

70
00:04:33,398 --> 00:04:37,598
that fits the task, inspect the recipient
carefully, and have

71
00:04:37,598 --> 00:04:40,858
the partner verify that the file opens
with the expected

72
00:04:40,858 --> 00:04:44,818
controls. A link that works for you may
still fail

73
00:04:44,818 --> 00:04:50,698
for someone else. Also distinguish
read-only from encryption. Read-only

74
00:04:50,698 --> 00:04:54,758
limits editing; it does not necessarily
prevent someone from reading

75
00:04:54,758 --> 00:04:59,098
or copying the information. Pause and
choose a role for

76
00:04:59,098 --> 00:05:03,138
someone who only needs to read the
finished handout. Viewer

77
00:05:03,138 --> 00:05:04,738
is the appropriate starting point.

78
00:05:06,495 --> 00:05:09,455
Sync is convenient because a change can
travel between a

79
00:05:09,455 --> 00:05:13,355
device and an online service. The same
convenience can carry

80
00:05:13,355 --> 00:05:16,975
a deletion. Watch the practice file
disappear from one location

81
00:05:17,575 --> 00:05:20,875
and then the other. That is why sync alone
is

82
00:05:20,875 --> 00:05:24,815
not a separate backup. A backup provides
another recovery copy

83
00:05:24,815 --> 00:05:28,515
under an appropriate plan. Version history
may preserve earlier

84
00:05:28,515 --> 00:05:31,935
content, while a recycle bin may retain a
deleted file

85
00:05:31,935 --> 00:05:36,335
for a limited time. Availability and
retention depend on the

86
00:05:36,335 --> 00:05:40,515
service. Do not test deletion with your
only important file.

87
00:05:41,375 --> 00:05:44,415
Use the fictional copy and explain which
protection would help

88
00:05:44,415 --> 00:05:47,255
if the mistake spread through every synced
location.

89
00:05:49,144 --> 00:05:52,404
First identify the problem. If the whole
file is missing

90
00:05:52,404 --> 00:05:57,004
after deletion, inspect the appropriate
trash or recycle bin. If

91
00:05:57,004 --> 00:06:00,684
the file exists but its wording changed,
inspect version history.

92
00:06:01,424 --> 00:06:04,604
Preview the earlier version and coordinate
with collaborators before

93
00:06:04,604 --> 00:06:08,004
restoring it. You do not want to replace
useful work

94
00:06:08,004 --> 00:06:11,544
someone just added. Compression is a
separate tool: a ZIP

95
00:06:11,544 --> 00:06:15,024
file can package several files for
transfer and may reduce

96
00:06:15,024 --> 00:06:18,764
their size. It is not automatically
encryption or a backup

97
00:06:18,764 --> 00:06:22,464
plan. After extracting a practice ZIP,
open a file to

98
00:06:22,464 --> 00:06:25,744
confirm the contents are usable. Choose
the tool that matches

99
00:06:25,744 --> 00:06:28,684
the problem rather than trusting a
familiar icon.

100
00:06:30,679 --> 00:06:33,919
Pause and build a small resource folder
for your chosen

101
00:06:33,919 --> 00:06:37,679
community task. Include a source note, a
fictional handout, and

102
00:06:37,679 --> 00:06:40,359
a clear file name. Tell a partner which
fact you

103
00:06:40,359 --> 00:06:44,379
verified, why that source is appropriate,
and which permission fits

104
00:06:44,379 --> 00:06:47,919
the review task. Then explain how you
would recover a

105
00:06:47,919 --> 00:06:51,459
deleted file and how that differs from
restoring earlier wording.

106
00:06:52,039 --> 00:06:55,259
If an account or sharing feature is
unavailable, demonstrate the

107
00:06:55,259 --> 00:06:59,199
decision with the course simulator and
label it simulated. For

108
00:06:59,199 --> 00:07:03,399
your next independent task, reuse this
sequence: ask a focused

109
00:07:03,399 --> 00:07:09,219
question, check the evidence, record a
trail, organize the file, and verify
access.
